@prefix chorus: <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

# =============================================================================
# #4045 — the nine products, in a file, on the IRIs the API serves.
#
# Until now every Product row (chorus:athena … chorus:werk, and spine minted
# 2026-09-01) existed ONLY in the live store: no deploy could reproduce them,
# and the #3603-era product-* rows this file used to hold were retired live but
# kept here, so the file described a graph that no longer existed. Same class of
# fix as #3838 (roles) and #4040 (pipelines): day-authored, in the manifest,
# seeded by athena-model into urn:chorus:instances under act — in the werk
# env at env-up and in prod at deploy-canonical. No hand runs.
#
# Jeff, 2026-09-02: "i expected all doc sections to be attributes in the class",
# then "we must do athena-* flow on these … using act … the pipeline". Spine
# carries its design-doc sections as the nine string properties #4045 added to
# ProductShape; the other eight fill theirs per card.
#
# Exported from the live store 2026-09-02 08:58 (dcterms provenance dropped —
# the seeder stamps its own), then spine's sections added. chorus:comment is
# emitted as both rdfs:comment (shape path) and chorus:comment (projection).
# =============================================================================

chorus:athena a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:comment """Athena is the structural and query layer of Chorus — the canonical model of what exists in the team's product world (products, domains, services, value-streams) and the predicates that connect them.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """Athena is the structural and query layer of Chorus — the canonical model of what exists in the team's product world (products, domains, services, value-streams) and the predicates that connect them.""" ;
    chorus:label """Athena""" ;
    rdfs:label """Athena""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    chorus:vision """A single SHACL-gated model whose one generator projects Product, Domain, Service, and ValueStream into uniform generated APIs and pages, made whole by construction through the DAL as the only write path.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """One queryable source of truth for the product tree with no fallback that yields a different answer, so every structural and impact question resolves to a real tree IRI rather than memory or pattern-matching.""" ;
    chorus:audience """Gates, SessionStart envelope injection, blast-radius, role-nudge routing, demo feedback, the tagging layer, and Jeff and the team via the rendered tree page.""" ;
    chorus:status """building""" ;
    chorus:gaps """A v1 predicate layer still being cleaned, the Service class not yet instantiated, and the generator not yet projecting inverse-path relationships as nested fields.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-designing ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-athena-product-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:products, chorus:services, chorus:domains, chorus:value-streams ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:promise """Athena is the model of what exists. It holds the canonical description of a product world — the products, the domains they are made of, the services that run inside those domains, and the value stream that carries work through them — as a graph with rules attached. Everything else in Chorus is placed in that graph: a principle, a pipeline, a log stream is a thing Athena knows about.
It is a design pipeline, not a schema. The structure (product, domain, service) is the product layer. The narrative on this page — the promise, the shape, the API, what stands and what is planned — is the design layer, stored on the model itself. The pipeline is what moves both: every entry is validated against the same rules, advances through one lifecycle (exploring, building, operating, retiring), and is shown by the same pages.
The promise: one source, many ways to ask, and no back door that gives a different answer. A question about structure or impact — who owns this, what depends on it, what is affected if it changes — resolves to a real entry in the model, never to someone's memory.""" ;
    chorus:structure """Athena is a product inside the Chorus platform, at the Designing step of the value stream. It is a leaf: it has no child products of its own.
It is made of four domains, one for each structural kind it governs: products, domains, services, and value streams. The page for each domain shows the full live set — the catalogue is generated, not maintained by hand.
Its surfaces are the four model verbs (model, validate, make, deploy — author it, check it, generate from it, ship it), the generated read and write APIs, the generated pages you are reading now, and a small set of tools that agents call: read the tree, look up an owner, compute the blast radius of a change.
Planned: overlays — annotations that are not containment, such as authority, ownership, deployment route, and customer satisfaction — queryable as their own relationships.""" ;
    chorus:model """The structural model lives at the value-stream level and is shared by every product: Product, Domain, Service, and ValueStream as classes; product and domain each able to contain more of their own kind; one relationship per meaning (a product has domains, consumes services, sits at a step, is owned by a role). Athena adds nothing structural of its own; it describes how the product serves its step and what surfaces it offers.
The rules are enforced where writes happen. Every entry must satisfy its shape before it is stored: required links must point at something that exists, a partial entry is refused whole rather than half-written, a retired relationship is refused by name with its replacement, and a required link cannot be removed. The generator only serves classes the model has claimed, so what is served is always a subset of what is declared.
In practice this means the model pushes back. When the spine product was first added it was refused four times until its gaps, its step, its design document and its domain were all real. That is the model doing its job.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """The pages are a walk through the model: value stream → step → product → domain → service. Every page is a rendering of its class's generated API plus the schema behind it, and every link on a page follows a real relationship in the model. Navigation starts at the value stream, not at a list of products.
Each page has the same anatomy: where you are, what this is, what it promises, a few numbers, the folds that hold its content (a fold always renders — if there is nothing in it, it says so), where each fact came from, and links that are relationships.
The product page you are reading shows every property the Product schema declares, present or not, with links resolved to the entries they point at, and reads on a phone: wide tables stack rather than scroll.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Generated from the model and served as versioned JSON. Every collection response names its API version, the model and vocabulary versions it was generated from, and the shape and commit behind it.
Read: /products and /products/{name}; /domains and /domains/{name} with their children; /services and /services/{name} with dependencies and consumers; /value-streams and /value-stream-steps; /documents. Each collection also serves an OpenAPI document (for example /products/openapi.json).
Write: create and replace on the same resources, through one governed door. A write needs an identity token, is validated against the shape, and is recorded with who wrote it and why. Replace drops properties the shape does not declare; there is no partial update.
Planned: a triple-pattern query (any subject, predicate, object), blast radius as a route rather than a tool, a read-only SPARQL passthrough, and overlay resources.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Serving today: the generated APIs and pages for products (nine), domains, services, value streams and documents; the four model verbs with a single governed write door; the agent tools for tree, ownership and blast radius; and the seed files that let the whole model be rebuilt from source into a fresh store, in a demo environment before a change lands and in production when it does.
Known limits: an older generation of relationships is still being retired one by one; the service layer is thin, with few services described; two vocabularies name the same things without a bridge; graph identifiers follow three naming conventions; and design documents are seeded into one graph while the document API reads another, so a fresh environment shows products without their design links.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """One generator treating product, domain, service and value stream identically, with the pages emitted by the generator rather than hand-built over its output.
The governed write door as the only way anything enters the model, with a guard that refuses the older direct deployer outside the pipeline.
A single pipeline trigger for model changes, the twin of the one used for code: author, validate, generate, deploy to a demo environment, show it, accept, deploy to production.
Every design document a first-class entry in the model, cited by identifier, and every product carrying its design narrative as attributes — this page is the first result of that.
Overlays as first-class annotations, one naming scheme for identifiers, and design documents seeded into the graph the API reads.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Reconciling the second vocabulary with the first — a platform-wide task, named here as a limit.
Gathering's own data domains (photos, music, stories) — they belong to that product's model; outside data enters through Convergence.
The card pipeline — it lives in the Clearing with its own events; Athena governs the lifecycle of artifacts, not of work items.
Observing the running system — Borg.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/athena-product-design.html (product design, 2026-05-15, restructured 2026-06-12); athena-value-stream-design.html and value-stream-template.html (the shared structural model).
Internal notes for the team — cards and decisions behind this page: ADR-040 (namespaces and identifier minting), ADR-028 (substrate-class domain contract), ADR-058 (model-only proving); reasoning-substrate-shift.html (2026-05-14); domain-model-asis-tobe.html; loom-subproduct-design.html (where the structural layer was first lifted); chorus-product-tree.html. Cards: #3345 / #3350 generated Domain API, #3351 value streams, #3257 the write door, #4045 products through the pipeline and design sections as attributes, #4080 this fill, #4062 the model pipeline trigger, #1772 one naming scheme, #4082 model versioning.
Chorus · Athena · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .

chorus:borg a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Borg""" ;
    rdfs:label """Borg""" ;
    chorus:comment """Borg is the self-judgment product of Chorus, spanning Proving and Reflecting — the system seeing itself so no human has to be the eyes.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """Borg is the self-judgment product of Chorus, spanning Proving and Reflecting — the system seeing itself so no human has to be the eyes.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-silas ;
    chorus:vision """A model-driven reflect cycle where monitor and alert are ontology domains whose contracts generate, the page renders a verdict not a readout, and the capability travels generatively to run with chorus off.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Borg's product isn't dashboards — it's Jeff's attention back: it surfaces the one thing that needs a person, calmly, to the owner, so no human is ever the first to notice a stall.""" ;
    chorus:audience """The roles (to self-coordinate and catch a dark peer, flapping service, or drift before Jeff does) and Jeff (outcomes only — a calm board, or the single thing that needs him).""" ;
    chorus:status """building""" ;
    chorus:gaps """Probes were bespoke and lied, there is no liveness or crash-loop signal, alerts are hand-routed, there is no model-driven contract, and there is no Borg page — the observation surfaces are scattered.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-proving ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-borg-product-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:infrastructure, chorus:builds, chorus:deploys, chorus:time, chorus:heralds, chorus:toolchain, chorus:analytics, chorus:security, chorus:Properties, chorus:alerts-monitors, chorus:logs, chorus:identity, chorus:metrics ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:promise """The system sees itself, so no person has to be the eyes. Borg watches everything it can reach — services, health, alerts, and the gap between how the system is drawn and how it actually runs — and surfaces the one thing that needs a person, calmly, to the person who owns it.
Its product is not dashboards. It is your attention back. Every time the owner is the first to notice a stall is a failure of this product. A service that froze silently for weeks is the anti-promise made concrete, and the reason Borg exists.
Who it is for: the roles building the system, who use it to notice a peer gone dark, a service flapping, a drift, before the owner does; and the owner, who consumes outcomes only — a calm board, or the single thing that needs them. Never a wall of warnings; never the job of monitor.""" ;
    chorus:structure """Borg spans the last two steps of the value stream with one eye at two focal lengths. Proving is per change: present it, gather feedback, reach a verdict, accept; it terminates. Reflecting is continuous: watch the running system and feed what it learns back into what gets built next; it loops.
Its domains group into three layers. Observability: heralds (the scouts that discover what is out there, including the crawlers), monitors, alerts, logs, metrics, analytics. Operations: builds, deploys, time (schedules), security and trust, identity. Infrastructure: the hosts, their properties, and the toolchain.
Owner: the architect role, wearing every hat — product, domain design, engineering lead, operations lead. Borg is not greenfield: a full observability stack already runs underneath it; the design names the cycle that runs over that stack.""" ;
    chorus:model """Monitors, alerts, service levels and signals are modeled as classes with rules, seated in the shared value-stream model. A monitor has a type (liveness, freshness, saturation, latency, errors, synthetic flow); an alert has severity, routing, and deduplication state; a service level has a burn rate.
The difference between the declared model and the observed behaviour is itself a modeled thing — the map versus the mirror — and it is what most Borg pages render. The map is the system drawing: how the system is meant to cohere. The mirror is how it runs. The gap between them is the signal.
The verdict at the front door is computed, not curated: calm when no critical alert is open, no service level is burning fast, and nothing monitored has gone silent past its freshness window; otherwise the single highest-severity unresolved signal.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """Every Borg page is generated from the model, not built by hand — the hand-built dashboards scattered across the stack are precisely the problem it replaces. You enter through the system drawing: the map. From the drawing, the Proving node opens the mirror.
The landing page is a verdict, not a readout. Two states: calm — "all coherent, nothing needs you — go" — or the one thing that does, alone, with nothing competing for the eye. You walk into a room and know instantly whether it is calm or on fire; if calm, you leave. The gauges are one level down, not the front door.
The reflect cycle renders as a seasonal wheel — plant, tend, read, surface, prune, feed — not a progress bar. Drill pages: monitors (which services are alive, which jobs ran, saturation, latency, errors, synthetic flows), alerts (what is firing and to whom it was routed), and health, metrics and fitness (the aggregate verdict, service-level burn, the flow report). The agent and the person read the same model, one through tools, the other through the generated routes.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Planned routes, generated from the monitor, alert and service-level classes:
GET /borg/verdict — the landing: calm, or the one thing, with the signal behind it.
GET /services/{name}/monitors — the typed monitor contract for a service. GET /services/{name}/liveness — alive-or-silent, from a heartbeat and an absence detector.
GET /alerts?owner=&severity= — what is firing, routed by ownership.
GET /slo/{name}/burn — service-level burn rate.
GET /borg/flow — the standing fitness report: cycle time, lead time, escaping defects, time to recover.
Serving today underneath: a Prometheus / Grafana / Loki / Alertmanager stack, health checks on a schedule, a log query surface by card or trace, a pain rollup of reactive work, and the nightly test run whose red list the roles read each morning.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Running: the full observability stack, a modeled monitor and alert taxonomy, and probes that stopped crying wolf. The nightly test run is the detector of a broken main branch.
Not yet: no liveness or crash-loop signal, which is why a frozen service once hid for weeks; alerts routed by hand rather than by ownership in the model; no model-driven monitor contract; and no Borg page — the observation surfaces are still scattered across the stack.
Recent lessons the product carries: a test suite once took down the very services it was observing, and went unnamed for weeks; a demo run from an isolated environment once wrote into production; a rollup of pain re-reports classes already fixed unless read day by day. Each is a monitor that should have spoken first.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """The reflect cycle, model-driven: monitors and alerts as domains whose contracts generate; the verdict-first landing page; silence and absence as first-class signals.
Path, in order: the value-stream spine, then liveness (the gap that hid the freeze), then freshness, errors, saturation, latency and synthetic flows, then ownership-routed alerting, then the pages that render it all.
Borg is built to travel. Chorus is a factory, and the systems it builds must run with Chorus switched off. So Borg ships generatively: the model generates a client system's own scrape configs, alert rules, service-level definitions, dashboards, liveness emitters and deploy scripts, plus a standalone open-source stack. The test is literal — turn Chorus off; does the client system still observe, alert and deploy itself? Any callback is a leaked dependency to cut. Borg is the proof that a modeled system can generate the commodity eighty percent every team otherwise rebuilds.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """The demo ceremony itself — presenting, gathering feedback, recording the verdict — belongs to Werk. Borg proves; Werk runs the ceremony.
Remediation in the reflecting half: Borg surfaces, the owning role acts. Its operations layer does deploy, roll back and restart; observe-only is a property of observability, not of the whole product.
Assimilation (extracting a legacy system's structure) and mitosis (reproducing the product for another system) — the other two Borg modes, described in their own designs; they share the scouts as their first step.
Triage of the work queue — a Directing concern.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/borg-product-design.html (2026-06-14) and borg-value-stream-design.html (the reflect cycle and the domains); athena-product-design.html is the mirrored template.
Internal notes for the team: ADR-043 (monitor / alert split, monitor types, service-level classes); observability-service-design.html, logs-service-design.html, infrastructure-service-design.html; chorus-product-domain-hierarchy.html; GOOD_BORG.html, BORG_ASSIMILATION_PATTERN.html, borg-mitosis.html. Cards: #3418 (the cycle, garden, map and mirror, verdict not readout), #3415 (design system), #3406 (the freeze that hid), #3405 (probe truthfulness), #3720 (the nightly black box), #4028 (role state derived from streams).
Chorus · Borg · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .

chorus:chorus a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Chorus""" ;
    rdfs:label """Chorus""" ;
    chorus:comment """The coordination protocol — hooks, scripts, gates, dashboards, spine, shared memory index. The nervous system.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """The coordination protocol — hooks, scripts, gates, dashboards, spine, shared memory index. The nervous system.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy """role-wren""" ;
    chorus:vision """A multi-agent/human team that builds a platform for building systems — coordination itself modeled, observable, improvable.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Three AI roles and one human work as a real team: shared memory, one board, one audit spine, gates at the right moments.""" ;
    chorus:audience """Jeff and the three roles, and future teams adopting the method.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """hasDesignDoc unfilled (no Document instances in graph). V1/V2 domain-layer convergence outstanding.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-directing ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-chorus-as-platform ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:search, chorus:memory ;
    chorus:promise """Chorus is a platform in the oldest sense: its product is not a feature but a method of building. It is a system that builds systems, and the people building inside it are AI agents working alongside one human. Everything it produces follows from one bet — get the way you build right, and what you build follows.
The proof that this is more than a slogan: the system generates large parts of itself. The model that describes Chorus is the model it uses to generate its own interfaces, tests and the scaffolding of its own actions, from one source. What is generated is the shape, the contract and the wiring; the logic inside is still written by hand.
If you adopted Chorus you would not be taking an app. You would be taking the method — a repeatable, instrumented, legible way to forge software with a small team of people and agents — and pointing it at your own streams of work. For the team inside it, Chorus is also shared memory: anyone can ask what happened last session, or what the team knows about a topic, and get a real answer without asking a person.""" ;
    chorus:structure """Chorus is a value stream that repeats: shape an idea, direct it, design it, build it, prove it, reflect, and shape the next thing. Each step carries a product's own stream inside it. Loom holds the operating model at Shaping. The Clearing, the Spine and Pulse are the team's input, record and vital signs at Directing. Athena is the model at Designing. Werk is the execution protocol at Building. Borg is self-observation across Proving and Reflecting. Convergence is the boundary where outside data comes in.
Under it all is a small grammar: nouns (a domain, a product, a piece of work), verbs (commit, deploy, register, run — a fixed, deliberately small set), and events (what a verb gives off when it fires — how the system sees itself). Nouns and verbs are peers, composed on need rather than in advance. Each element performs many functions; each function is supported by many elements.
The team is a few persistent AI roles, each defined by what it is accountable for, plus one human owner. Each role runs its own streams and helps build the shared platform every stream uses. The domains Chorus itself carries are memory and search — the shared memory substrate.""" ;
    chorus:model """One model for the whole platform: value stream, step, product, domain, service, with product and domain able to contain their own kind. Each class has a shape that says what an entry must carry, and one governed door is the only way to write. The model is reproducible from source: roles, value streams, products, documents and pipelines are seed files that deploy through the same pipeline as code.
One standard, many owners. Each domain's owner defines that domain's own data, because the person closest to it knows it best. What is held in common is the standard every definition must meet — the shapes, the identifier discipline, the contract for what an action emits — not the definitions themselves. The pattern avoided on purpose is one architect designing everyone's data.
A domain is a class in the model. A verb has a contract. The repository mirrors the model's recursion.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """The method is a cycle, and the pages follow it. The system drawing at /chorus is the map. From it: the value stream, then the nine products as tiles, then a product's page (what you are reading — hero, vision, chapters), then its domains and services. Every page renders its class's generated API; every link is a relationship in the model.
The Clearing is where the team is watched working in real time: role tiles, the message stream, session tails, alerts, and a group chat — from any device.
The Loom pages render the operating model: principles, and the policies each of them justifies.
Search runs over the team's whole memory — sessions, events, briefs, decisions, chat — and is the first place a question goes before anyone reads a file.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """One front door for programmatic access, the Chorus API:
Search: /api/chorus/search across thirteen source types, hybrid keyword and semantic; /api/chorus/health; /api/chorus/freshness (how fresh each source is).
Context: /api/chorus/context/roles, /health, /alerts, /board/wip, /spine — the same envelope every agent receives before each turn.
Knowledge: /api/chorus/knowledge/domains and /search over the graph.
Operating model: /api/loom/principles, /api/loom/policies.
The generated model APIs (products, domains, services, value streams, documents, tests) — see Athena.
For agents: one typed tool per verb of the pipeline and the model, plus tools for the board, messaging, logs and principles; the tool surface is pinned by a contract test so adding one is a deliberate act. A hook layer intercepts every agent action to enforce policy, inject context and record what happened.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Running: the Chorus API; a keyword index fed continuously from sessions and events, and a semantic index beside it; per-turn context injection into every agent; an append-only event log that is never rotated; the Clearing; the board and its command-line client; the code pipeline with a demo stop before anything lands; the generated model APIs; and a nightly test run.
The honest frontier is self-regulation. The four lenses Chorus draws on — permaculture, team topologies, cybernetics, the rhizome — all describe a system that holds its own health. Chorus has the structure of that system but not yet the reflex: when something drifts, a person still tends to notice first.
Other limits: several data sources (code, tests, commits, logs, state files, skills, the board) have no ingestion path into memory; freshness alerting is binary; and delivering a message into a live agent session remains the flakiest part of the whole platform.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Close the reflex gap: the system senses and corrects its own operational health before a person has to — verdict-first observation, liveness signals, role state derived from what roles actually do rather than what they declare.
Everything through the pipeline: code, the model, and the daily rhythm through one uniform verb interface, with off-the-shelf conductors (a build runner, a scheduler, a data-flow engine) rather than an orchestration engine of its own.
Every design document a first-class entry in the model, with the pages rendered from it — this page is the first of nine.
Memory complete: ingestion for the missing sources, graduated freshness, a rebuild endpoint.
The commercial shape: a client takes the method, not an app. The factory generates the commodity substrate; human craft goes to what is different about the product. Gathering, the personal application Chorus was born from, is client zero.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Gathering's application features — Gathering is what Chorus builds and its first client; its own data belongs to its own model.
The internals of each product — Athena, Borg, Clearing, Convergence, Loom, Pulse, Spine and Werk each carry their own design on their own page.
Hosted continuous integration — retired for cost; the merge gate is a local pipeline run plus the owner's go, and the nightly run is the detector.
A bespoke orchestration engine — Chorus builds the connective tissue once and rents the conductor.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/chorus-as-platform.html ("A System to Build Systems" — the method, the grammar, the cycle, how the team is composed, the four influences, the honest frontier) and chorus-service-design.html (the shared-memory substrate).
Internal notes for the team: chorus-reference-model.html, why-and-how-of-chorus.html, system-model-thinking.html, cybernetic-systems-chorus.html, chorus-nervous-system.html, HOMEOSTASIS_RESEARCH.html; value-stream-design.html, value-stream-template.html, chorus-value-stream-deep-dive.html, chorus-spine.html; self-model-owl-framework.html, owl-spec-as-source-flow.html, chorus-product-domain-hierarchy.html; chorus-wardley.html, chorus-consulting.html. Decisions: ADR-032 (verb contract), ADR-041 (recursive repo), ADR-045 (a domain is a class), ADR-046 (emit contract), ADR-053 (merge gate), DEC-074 (search first), DEC-093 (one API door).
Chorus · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" .

chorus:clearing a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """The Clearing""" ;
    rdfs:label """The Clearing""" ;
    chorus:comment """Multi-party real-time alignment — browser chat with Jeff and all three AI roles. Parent product of Spine and Pulse.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """Multi-party real-time alignment — browser chat with Jeff and all three AI roles. Parent product of Spine and Pulse.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    chorus:vision """One shared room where the team aligns in minutes instead of relaying briefs, with every DECISION: line landing in the graph automatically.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Real-time multi-party alignment with decisions captured where they happen — Jeff stops being the transport layer between role terminals.""" ;
    chorus:audience """Jeff and all three roles during alignment moments. Borg reads the transcripts, and decisions marked in-channel are captured to the decision log.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """hasDesignDoc unfilled (no Document instances in graph). Transcript indexing depends on the index-freshness pipeline that alerts today.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-directing ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-clearing-value-stream-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:cards, chorus:streams, chorus:messages ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:promise """The Clearing is where the team and the running system meet. It has three faces: the board (input — what we choose to do), the messages (output — how something reaches a person or an agent session), and the record (what happened). Your choices on the board and the system's own events land in the same log.
For the owner, the promise reads like this: I see the team working in real time. Tiles show who is building what; the stream shows decisions and demos as they happen; alerts surface without my checking terminals. When the Clearing works, I manage by glancing. When it breaks, I am back to watching three terminals and guessing who is stuck.
The Clearing is a substrate, not a pipeline. Every other product borrows it: the standard verbs for nudging a role, surfacing a message into a session, and injecting context are the Clearing's, and every action's trace ends up in its record.""" ;
    chorus:structure """The Clearing sits at the Directing step. It is made of the domains cards (the board: work items, groupings, tags, states, limits on work in progress), messages (nudges between roles, the bridge into sessions, group chat) and streams (each role's live session, tailed). Pulse, the team's vital signs, is its child product; the Spine, the event log, grew out of it into a product of its own.
Its verbs fall by face. Board: file a card, group cards, tag them, rank groups by value, order cards by dependency (a separate axis from value — a valuable card can sit behind a cheap prerequisite), sweep for stale or duplicate items, show what is in flight. Messages: nudge a role (always both persisted and delivered), surface a message into a session, inject context, gather peer input. Record: tail the log.
Ownership splits by layer: the model and coordination with the product role, the presentation with the engineering role, the log substrate with the architect.""" ;
    chorus:model """A Kanban flow whose system of record is an append-only event log. Cards are pulled through Next, In progress and Done under limits on work in progress; every board action and every message appends an event; the board you see is a projection built by folding those events. The board is a read model; the log is the truth.
The content classes: a Card, with its tags across four axes and the acceptance criteria and experience statement that must be present before it can be pulled; a Message or Nudge, with a from/to envelope, persisted and delivered every time; a Stream, one per live session; and role state, derived from what a role's session is actually doing rather than from anything a role declares.
The Clearing was the first service described in the model itself; its service page is generated from that entry and may not contradict it. Two rules govern it: a nudge is always both stored and delivered, and nothing is done until it has been deployed, shown, and accepted.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """The Clearing page has six parts: role tiles (state, current card, last action, for the owner and each role), the message stream (classified — the owner's input, demos, blocks and decisions are visible; protocol noise is hidden), session tails (each role's terminal, collapsible), an alerts panel (by severity), a group chat (with a prefix that auto-captures decisions, image and voice upload), and remote access (token on the public tunnel, guests by name, nothing required on the local network).
Every demo environment runs its own Clearing on its own ports, pointed at that environment's API, so a test can never post into the owner's real room.
The flow: a card is filed (by the owner directly, or proposed by an agent and approved) → pulled, which creates an isolated workspace → built, and the pipeline's events (pulled, demoed, accepted) enter the log → the tiles and stream render the projection → nudges carry the handoffs.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Board: a command-line client (list, view, add, move, done, set, tag) and matching agent tools; the board's work-in-progress view by role at /api/chorus/context/board/wip.
Messages: a messaging service that persists and drains history, and one agent tool that both persists a nudge and delivers it into the recipient's live session over whichever transport that session uses; /api/chorus/context/roles for state.
The Clearing server: a message endpoint the bridge and alerts post to, a sessions endpoint, real-time push for tiles, stream and chat, a health route; administration local-only.
Record: one emit command that appends an event, and query tools that read the log by card, by trace, or for recent errors; /api/chorus/context/spine for the tail.
Context: before every agent turn, a hook drains the message queue and the current pulse, events and model context into that turn.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Live and proven end to end: a phone on the public tunnel reaching a locked-screen agent session over the terminal-multiplexer transport. Outbound delivery to agents rides the session registry across three transport types. The bridge token is enforced on tunneled traffic, and the Clearing refuses unauthenticated callers from the network.
Running: the board on its backing service and command-line client; the append-only log; the nudge path and the injection binary; a Clearing per demo environment; role state derived from the streams.
Limits: no per-participant identity on the public door — anyone with the link and token reaches the room; export is unreliable; delivery into an agent session is terminal injection rather than subscription; the verb family is named in the design but not yet modeled, so its surfaces are not generated; and a set of browser tests skip until each demo environment's room is wired in.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Model the verb family, so the board and message surfaces are generated from the model like every other product's.
Harden delivery, because every product borrows it: receipts for received and acted-on, retry on silence, a from/to envelope on every nudge.
A live pipeline strip on the work-in-progress view — the first tile that projects a trace from the log.
The board itself rendered as a projection of the model and the log rather than a separate application, with a hierarchy (value stream → domain → product → work) replacing flat labels.
Per-participant identity on the public door, and a real browser running the owner's loop against the live Clearing before any change to it lands.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """What goes on the board and in what order — the owner's and the product manager's call; the Clearing carries the board, it does not decide it.
The log as a product of its own (event types, occurrences, how far back a reader can reach) — the Spine.
Aggregation, dashboards and alert rules — Borg.
The composition of the vital signs — Pulse.
Who a participant is — the identity layer; the Clearing consumes it.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/clearing-value-stream-design.html (the three faces, the verb family, the event-sourced engine, the domains — 2026-06-21) and clearing-service-design.html (the six components, supporting domains, infrastructure — 2026-04-06, refreshed 2026-07-23).
Internal notes for the team: value-stream-template.html; streams-service-design.html, cards-service-design.html, nudge-service-design.html, service-design-pulse.html. Decisions: DEC-107 (persist and deliver, both, always), DEC-048 (deploy, demo, accept), DEC-093 (one API door), DEC-1571 (announce and continue), ADR-039 (transports). Cards: #3544 / #3548 (surface hardening), #3668 (multiplexer transport), #3669 (auth lanes), #3674 (relay spike), #4075 (a Clearing per demo environment), #4028 (derived role state), #3966 (closed to the network), #2159 (native board), #2708 / #3363 / #3112 (delivery receipts, retry, envelope), #3871 (prod canary).
Chorus · The Clearing · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .

chorus:convergence a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Convergence""" ;
    rdfs:label """Convergence""" ;
    chorus:comment """Cross-domain integration — ICDs, harvesters, data pipelines. The product whose job is making the many sources one graph.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """Cross-domain integration — ICDs, harvesters, data pipelines. The product whose job is making the many sources one graph.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-kade ;
    chorus:vision """Every outside source lands through one governed ingestion path — mapped, reconciled, and conformed to the canonical model before it enters the graph, so nothing arrives unshaped.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Harvesters and pipelines governed by ICDs — every integration is declared before it is coded, so data lineage is a contract rather than an accident.""" ;
    chorus:audience """The roles as integration authors, the graph as the destination, the ICD gate as the enforcement point.""" ;
    chorus:status """building""" ;
    chorus:gaps """hasDesignDoc unfilled (no Document instances in graph). V1/V2 domain IRI duplication outstanding. Change-management engine (impact, propagate, verify) not yet built.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-building ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-convergence-value-stream-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:integrations ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:promise """Convergence is the boundary. It takes data from outside sources — an existing system, a legacy schema, an external feed — and brings it into the model, reconciled and conformed. Athena projects the model outward; Convergence pulls the world inward. They share one membrane, the canonical model, approached from opposite sides.
Three verbs: ingest, map, land. Triggered by incoming data, one batch at a time, each batch produced and finished. The verb that carries the weight is land, and its heart is reconciliation, not writing. Thirty years of integration work reduce to one rule: always reconcile before loading, because source data never matches cleanly. A load that skips reconciliation fills the model with dirty, duplicated, unconformed data and corrupts what it was meant to enrich.
Convergence is also where a discovered system's structure lands: when Borg's scouts map an existing system, this is the path by which what they found enters the graph.""" ;
    chorus:structure """A line, and the inbound twin of Athena. The ingestion belongs to the engineering role: the harvesters, the ingest–map–reconcile pipeline, source registrations, and the extraction of Chorus's own history from the application it was born in. The canonical model the data must conform to, and the contract gate in front of it, belong to the architect; the shapes and the design belong to the product role.
Today it carries one domain, integrations. The design names five that follow the pipeline: sources (registered inbound systems and their native schemas), interface contracts (the provider and consumer agreement per domain, enforced by a gate), harvesters (the ingest workers, live for the personal application's data), mappings (source schema to canonical model transforms), and reconciliation (cleanse, deduplicate, resolve entities to a golden record, keep the trail of which source fed which row).
Where it recurses in the value stream is still open — a harvesting step of its own, or across steps. It is the least-drafted of the six streams.""" ;
    chorus:model """Tracked data units flowing through a transform graph into a reconciled system of record. Data arrives as explicit packets that carry their provenance; it is routed and translated toward one canonical form; before it lands it is reconciled against what is already there.
Transform-before-land versus land-raw-then-transform is a mapping decision; given the model's validation floor, transforming first is the likely default. Conformance to the canonical model is not negotiable; where the transform runs is.
Provenance is first-class: every landed fact retraces to its source, which is essential for a system that absorbs other systems and must explain where each fact came from. Reconciliation combines format normalization (standard codes, canonical keys), cleansing, deduplication, and entity resolution into one golden record plus the source-to-row mapping.
The gate at the door: no harvester code without a matching interface contract. The contract precedes the code; the mapping precedes the load.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """Designed, not yet built. Convergence has no page of its own today. What can be read are Athena's page for the integrations domain, the interface-contract notes and comparison, a strategy map of the contract layer, and the crawler dependency map.
The intended flow: a source is registered → its interface contract is written and validated → a harvester ingests tracked units with provenance → mapping routes and translates toward the canonical model → reconciliation conforms, cleanses, deduplicates and resolves entities → land writes conformant entries into the graph, each traceable to its origin. Each stage becomes a generated page over its class, like every other domain.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Today: the generated read for the integrations domain; the interface-contract graph, readable through the triple store; the harvesters for the personal application's data; and a flow engine on a second machine for the photo pipeline, currently down and slated for revival.
Planned: one pipeline trigger mirroring the code and model triggers — ingest, map, land per batch, with typed refusals at the reconciliation gate; generated routes for sources, contracts, harvesters, mappings and reconciliation; and a provenance query: which sources fed this row.
Guarded now: the interface-contract gate on harvester writes, and a membrane that refuses writes to production surfaces from test or build contexts.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Convergence exists mostly as intent and a few running pieces, not as a built pipeline. The interface-contract gate runs. The harvesters for the personal application run. The photo pipeline's flow engine is down and awaiting revival. The domain entry in the model is present with one domain, in the building state.
What the design settled: reconcile before load as the non-negotiable; Convergence as the inbound twin of Athena; and the factory rule — what Convergence ingests must never become a runtime dependency of the products Chorus builds. The test is literal: turn Chorus off, and the application it feeds must still start.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Write the value stream into the model: Convergence and its three steps as first-class entries.
Contract first: no harvester without a matching interface contract; the contract is the floor of the write door.
One source end to end: pick one inbound domain and run ingest, map, reconcile and land all the way through, proving the reconciliation gate. The rest of Convergence is that, repeated.
Golden records with provenance: entity resolution to one canonical record, and a mapping from every source row to it, so every landed fact retraces to its origin.
Generate the mappings where possible, so the source-to-canonical transform is itself a model artifact and cannot drift from the schema.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """The canonical model itself — Athena's product and the architect's floor; Convergence lands into it.
The application logic over ingested data — the consuming product's.
Discovering an existing system's structure — Borg's scouts; Convergence lands what they find.
A live coupling between the personal application and Chorus — refused by design; the relationship is ingest-and-conform.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/convergence-value-stream-design.html (the ingest–map–land cycle, the engine, the five domains, sequencing — 2026-06-21) and CONVERGENCE_ARCHITECTURE.html (the earlier architecture).
Internal notes for the team: value-stream-template.html; athena-value-stream-design.html (the outbound twin); ICD_PROCESS_FLOW.html, ICD_COMPARE_NOTES.html, ICD_TEST_STRATEGY.html, WARDLEY_MAP_ICD.html; crawler-dependency-map.html, crawler-service-design.html; BORG_ASSIMILATION_PATTERN.html. Reading: Flow-Based Programming (Morrison); Apache NiFi; Enterprise Integration Patterns (Hohpe and Woolf); record linkage and entity resolution. Decisions: DEC-095 (contract gate; mapper before harvest), DEC-096. Cards: #3392, #2291, #3368 (revive the flow engine), #3615 (membrane).
Chorus · Convergence · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .

chorus:loom a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Loom""" ;
    rdfs:label """Loom""" ;
    chorus:comment """The operating-model and team-knowledge layer of Chorus — the team's persistent product memory at the Shaping step — carrying principles, practices, policies, decisions, roles, skills, and RCAs.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """The operating-model and team-knowledge layer of Chorus — the team's persistent product memory at the Shaping step — carrying principles, practices, policies, decisions, roles, skills, and RCAs.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-jeff ;
    chorus:vision """A coherent product modeled on the recursive Athena model, one clean predicate set, every content type a real OWL class with a SHACL shape, every subdomain populated or explicitly planned — Loom as the template the other products follow.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Loom gives the team one coherent, validated home for its normative substrate as real OWL classes with SHACL shapes, so the operating model is graphed and constrained rather than scattered as ungraphed markdown.""" ;
    chorus:audience """The Chorus team — the three AI roles and Jeff — for whom Loom holds the persistent operating frame; secondarily any future team adopting Chorus, who receives a working normative model rather than a pile of docs.""" ;
    chorus:status """exploring""" ;
    chorus:gaps """5 of 7 intent subdomains are empty shells (decisions live as ungraphed markdown; Decision and RCA aren't OWL classes); the predicate layer needs cleaning; content is unconstrained by SHACL.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-shaping ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-loom-subproduct-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:rcas, chorus:roles, chorus:principles, chorus:policies, chorus:practices, chorus:skills, chorus:decisions ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:promise """Loom is the operating model — the team's persistent memory of how it works. It holds the normative layer: the principles the team stands on, the practices that express them, the policies that enforce them, the decisions it has made, the roles it is made of, the skills those roles can invoke, and the analyses of what went wrong.
The promise is coordination without a switchboard. A few AI agents and one person work together because the rules of working are written down, enforced where actions happen, and fed back from experience — not because a person relays every message and remembers every rule.
Loom is a loop at the Shaping step. Principles are the source; practices express them; policies enforce them through gates and hooks; and what the running system teaches — a post-mortem, a retrospective, a correction from the owner — flows back into the principles. Every policy names the principle that justifies it, and the live principle set is placed in front of every agent at the start of every session.""" ;
    chorus:structure """Loom is a product inside Chorus at the Shaping step, in the exploring state. It is made of exactly seven domains: principles, practices, policies, decisions, roles, skills, and root-cause analyses. An earlier audit found the graph had grown to twelve, with metrics, analytics and several service nodes attached where they did not belong; it has since been cut back to the seven that were intended.
What those domains hold: twenty-eight principles (drawn from Extreme Programming's values and from permaculture design, each with the team's own reading); the practices that express them; fifteen policies, most of them rules enforced at the end of an agent's turn, such as a cap on reply length and a plain-language requirement; the decision record; four roles, each a bundle of five hats; the invokable skills; and the analyses.
The product role authors the normative layer and owns the roles domain; the architect owns the analyses and the coherence of the model itself.""" ;
    chorus:model """The audit that shaped Loom found the relationships between things were the least coherent layer of the whole model — one link declared twice with different meanings, another overloaded into a second job, a third used dozens of times and never defined, and two of Loom's content types not even declared as classes. Fixing that produced the structural model every product now uses: product and domain each able to contain their own kind, one link per meaning, and a shape for every class.
Loom's content classes, one per domain, each with a shape: a Principle (name, body, owner); a Practice (which must express at least one principle); a Policy (which must be enforced by at least one gate or hook); a Decision (typed as a decision or an architecture record, dated); an Analysis (incident, root cause, date); a Role (agent or human); a Skill (composed of some mix of agent, tool, gate and hook). One rule across all seven: every entry belongs to exactly one domain — no orphan principles, no orphan policies.
Today the principles and policies are served from the model with the policy-to-principle link real; decisions still live largely as text awaiting their API.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """The principles page lists all twenty-eight, live from the model; the same set is injected into every agent session at boot, so no agent works from a stale copy. The policies page lists the fifteen rules, each crediting its principle.
Roles appear as tiles whose state is derived from what each role is actually doing. Decisions are readable on the documentation site and through search. Skills are invoked by name from the terminal; there is no catalogue page for them yet.
The loop, as a flow: a principle is minted → practices and policies cite it → hooks enforce the policies at the moment an agent acts or finishes a turn → an analysis or a correction from the owner proposes a change to the principle set → the loop closes at Shaping. Nine patterns of interaction with the owner (direction, ideation, demo, triage, emergency, observation, alignment, story, reflection) are detected and recorded as events so the system can reflect on how the team actually works.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Read: /api/loom/principles (the live set, also the boot-envelope source); /api/loom/policies (each with its principle); the generated domain reads for principles, practices, policies, decisions, roles, skills and analyses as their classes land in the shared model; a flow-metrics route over the event log.
For agents: list, read and create principles; list and read decisions; register feedback; a pain rollup of reactive work; and a live-observation tool that watches a role working.
Enforcement is the hook layer: a set of rules that run before an agent acts, after it acts, when the owner submits a prompt, and when the agent finishes a turn. The policies are executable there, not merely written down.
Planned: invoke-at-the-decision-point — pass a work item and a decision point, get back the principles and practices that apply — and a decisions API so the record leaves text files for good.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Served from the model: twenty-eight principles, injected at every session start; fifteen policies, each crediting its principle; the seven intended domains; the four roles as reproducible entries with their state derived from their live sessions.
Still narrative rather than structured: the decision record — two hundred and more decisions and architecture records in text, with no API and no check that a decision is actually enforced (one delivery rule was broken for weeks before anyone noticed). Skills are files, not queryable, with no catalogue. Some gates once fired falsely often enough to teach agents to route around them; the root causes were addressed but trust is rebuilt slowly.
Detection of the nine interaction patterns is done by the product role by hand, not by a hook.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Decisions in the model: a Decision class with its shape, an API for creating and changing them, and the text file retired as the record — every decision cited by identifier.
Skills queryable: skill entries with their composition, a registry and a catalogue the owner can browse, and validation of how a skill is invoked.
Analyses modeled: incident, root cause, date — the learning corpus that feeds candidate principles, so recurring incoherence becomes a rule.
Invoke at the decision point: the principles and practices that apply to this work item at this step, not a list dump.
Policies that prove themselves: every gate ships with a demonstration that it fails when it should, and its refusal names its state; pattern detection becomes a hook rather than a habit.
Loom as template: the coherence audit that shaped it — a defined upward link, one containment relationship, every domain either populated or marked planned, real classes with shapes, one vocabulary — repeated across the other products.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """The structural layer (product, domain, service, containment) — lifted out of Loom into Athena and owned there.
The second vocabulary and the duplicated graphs the audit named — platform-wide, not the Loom slice.
The Clearing's surfaces and the board — the Clearing; an early Loom service design listed them before the products were separated.
Running the gates at demo time — Werk; Loom defines the rules the gates enforce.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/loom-subproduct-design.html (the coherence audit and the resulting model — 2026-05-14) and loom-service-design.html (the nine components, deployed versus missing — 2026-04-05); loom-value-stream-design.html (the Shaping loop).
Internal notes for the team: chorus-reference-model.html (the earliest Loom definition); roles-service-design.html, roles-domain-evolution.html; skill-lifecycle.html; INTERACTION_PATTERNS.md; /loom/principles.html; the principle seed at platform/api/src/sparql/seeds/permaculture-principles.rq. Decisions: ADR-045 (a domain is a class). Cards: #4077 (policies credit principles; plain language), #3838 (roles as seed entries), #4028 (derived role state), #2318 (decisions API), #3734 (no gate without a negative proof), #2152 (decisions into the graph).
Chorus · Loom · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .

chorus:pulse a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Pulse""" ;
    rdfs:label """Pulse""" ;
    chorus:comment """The team's vital signs — who is working, on what, and what they are waiting on. A child product of The Clearing.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """The team's vital signs — who is working, on what, and what they are waiting on. A child product of The Clearing.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    chorus:vision """Jeff can look once and know where the work stands. Who is moving, who is stuck, and what is waiting on him — read in a glance, never assembled by asking three people.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Coordination is the tax on a team of three agents and one human. Pulse pays it down by making the team's state readable instead of askable: the roles watch each other through it rather than messaging to check, and Jeff stops being the one who notices a stall. Its raw material is what the team already emits while working, so being legible costs a role nothing extra.""" ;
    chorus:audience """Jeff first — one screen, no interpretation required. The three roles second, as the way they see each other without interrupting each other. Alerting third, as a consumer of the same reading.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """Pulse can say who a role CLAIMS to be, not what they are doing — so a role deep in work can read as idle, and the reading Jeff most needs is the one least trustworthy. It has no notion of waiting: it lists roles and cards but not who is blocked on whom, which is the question that actually costs a coordination day. It reports the same way on a loaded machine as an idle one, so a slow reading and a stalled role look identical. And it is read-only by nature — it can show a stall but cannot route it, so noticing still depends on someone looking.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-directing ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-product-design-pulse ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:knowledge ;
    chorus:consumes chorus:code, chorus:logs, chorus:roles, chorus:alerts-monitors, chorus:messages, chorus:streams, chorus:events, chorus:cards, chorus:security, chorus:memory, chorus:knowledge, chorus:tests ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:promise """You look once and know whether your team is working — without asking anyone, and without being lied to. That is the whole product. Its measure is not uptime or latency; it is how many times in a day the owner has to type "are you working?" The day this product was designed the answer was six, and each time the answer was already in the log.
Who it is for: the owner, at a glance, on a phone, often away from the local network — the one consumer whose time is irreplaceable, so a reading they cannot reach is a reading that does not exist. The roles, for mutual observation: read a peer's state rather than message them to ask. Alerting, as a consumer and never an owner: alerts read the pulse; the pulse never decides what an alert may say.
The metaphor is the owner's: I check it the way you check a pulse. Am I alive? Is anything stuck? Is something spiking?""" ;
    chorus:structure """Pulse is a product, not a domain, and a child of the Clearing, in the operating state. For a while one identifier carried both meanings, so two pages answered differently about the same thing; the domain typing was retired and the product kept.
Its domain is the short-term layer of memory — what is true right now and decays — alongside long-term memory (search) and knowledge (the model). Three layers: what is happening now, injected into every agent turn; what happened recently, on the Clearing's ambient panel; what happened ever, in search.
Two rhythms. The heartbeat is periodic: daily reviews, the operations agent, the nightly test run — signals that prove the system is alive, so a missed beat is itself a signal. The nerve impulse is event-driven: a card pulled or accepted, a nudge delivered, a role's state changed, a gate blocking an action, a deploy finished, an alert firing, a session starting or ending.
The composition: board, health, alerts, events, and role state, assembled into one durable reading.""" ;
    chorus:model """Pulse is a read-through cache with freshness, not an aggregator. Every section has its own producer and its own cadence; the pulse reads each, annotates it with its age, and exposes it. It never decides what a consumer may see.
The product bet: observation beats declaration. The system already emits everything needed to answer "who is doing what" — an activity beat every ten seconds from each agent, with its phase, its current tool, elapsed time and session. A state derived from what a role did cannot go stale the way a state a role promised to update does. Well over a hundred work items were spent on better ways for a role to declare its state, and the state field was still wrong when it mattered. The fix was not another declaration path; it was deriving state from the stream, which is now how it works.
A signal hierarchy keeps attention safe: critical signals interrupt (a service down, a deploy failure, an alert); important ones appear ambiently (a card moved, a demo started); informational ones are only logged. The rule for what counts as pulse: events that say what state the system just entered, not what action someone performed.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """Where the reading appears: the Clearing's role tiles (state, current card, last action), its ambient events panel and alerts panel; the pulse block at the top of every agent's turn (health, work in progress, index freshness); a single durable file for anything that needs the whole stack; and the dashboards for the trend.
The flow: something happens → one emit command appends a structured event to the log → the assembler folds board, health, alerts, events and the derived role states into one reading, cached by section so it is fast → tiles, the agent envelope and the API read it with its age → the owner glances.
Wanted and not yet built: a literal pulse line on the Clearing that flatlines when no events flow; a reading reachable away from the local network; and "who is blocked on whom" in one hop.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Read: /api/chorus/context/roles (every role, derived state, current card); /api/chorus/context/health; /api/chorus/context/alerts; /api/chorus/context/board/wip; /api/chorus/context/spine (the recent tail); /api/chorus/freshness (how fresh each memory source is). The assembled reading is also available as one call, with per-section timings.
The model entry behind this page: the generated product read for Pulse.
Write: one emit command is the only way an event enters the log; the only state a role still declares by hand is "blocked", with a reason; everything else is derived. An activity beat every ten seconds from each agent is the raw material.
Consumers: the Clearing tiles, the per-turn agent envelope, alerting, and the roles observing one another.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Working: the whole stack assembles in well under a second; emission to the log is continuous; board, health, alerts and events are all present in the reading; alert suppression that once hid signals has been removed.
Fixed since the design was written: role state used to be wrong by construction — the declared value always won and inference could only annotate it. State is now derived from the activity stream; the declared file is gone; the tiles read only the derived value.
Still open from the same measurement: no per-source timestamps or schema version in the reading, and no consumer reads an age; role messages appear as a count rather than content; no relation between roles, so "who is blocked on whom" cannot be answered; machine load is absent, so a reading taken under heavy load looks like one taken at rest; and a share of activity beats arrive without a known author, which limits how much can be derived.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """The target column of the design: the owner asks "are you working?" zero times a day; declared-versus-derived disagreement measured daily and trending to zero; every activity beat bound to a known principal; a reading reachable away from the local network; blocked-on-whom answerable in one hop.
Derivation all the way down: relations between roles from nudges and blocked declarations; machine load in the reading; per-source ages that consumers actually use.
The signal hierarchy enforced at delivery, so only critical signals steal focus; a missed-heartbeat event for every periodic producer; a pulse visualization on the Clearing; recent events by urgency as an API.
Two questions the design left open have answers: who watches the watchers — Borg's liveness leg; and is Borg the brain that interprets the pulse — yes, the pulse is the raw signal Borg reasons over.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Cards, messages, alerts and events — each lives in its own domain; Pulse holds only the hot register over each.
Persistence — anything worth keeping belongs to long-term memory.
Identity — who a principal is belongs to the identity layer; Pulse reports only what that principal did.
Deciding what an alert may say — alerting is a consumer.
Search queries, file edits, commits, the owner's typing, tool-call logs — retrieval, output, input and telemetry, not pulse.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/product-design-pulse.html (the experience, who it is for, product not domain, the bet, what good looks like — 2026-08-25) and service-design-pulse.html (two rhythms, the event catalogue, memory layers, signal hierarchy, the measured state — 2026-03-31, refreshed 2026-08-25).
Internal notes for the team: chorus-pulse-asis.html and chorus-pulse-tobe.html; pulse-service-design.md (read-through cache with freshness, 2026-04-21); memory-attention-awareness.html, MEMORY_ARCHITECTURE.html. Owner's words: "pulse is a product not a domain" and "derive state from streams" (2026-08-25); the focus problem (2026-03-31). Cards: #4010 (the designs; domain typing retired), #4028 (derived role state), #2442 (alert suppression), #3202 (durable reading), #1887 (the bridge layer), #3613 (identity).
Chorus · Pulse · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .

chorus:spine a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Spine""" ;
    rdfs:label """Spine""" ;
    chorus:comment """Two layers, not one (Jeff 2026-09-01, EDA framing): EventType is curated vocabulary in the graph; SpineEvent is the log projected on read. Composes the events domain; the spine vocabulary domain shares its IRI (#1772) and cannot be linked until one side is renamed. Excludes logs (Silas, Proving) — a consumer, not a constituent.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """Two layers, not one (Jeff 2026-09-01, EDA framing): EventType is curated vocabulary in the graph; SpineEvent is the log projected on read. Composes the events domain; the spine vocabulary domain shares its IRI (#1772) and cannot be linked until one side is renamed. Excludes logs (Silas, Proving) — a consumer, not a constituent.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    chorus:vision """A team that can replay its own history can improve deliberately — event types as curated vocabulary, occurrences as an immutable stream, joined by ofType.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """The team memory: an append-only record of everything that happened, and a catalogue of everything that can happen. Nothing is lost, anything recorded is reachable, and what can happen is enumerable.""" ;
    chorus:audience """Jeff, the three roles, and every other product — Werk, Borg, Pulse, Loom and Athena all read it.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """Reach: readers take a fixed 4MB tail = the last 3h17m of a 40-day 1.71GB log, so promise 2 is broken today. EventType is unmodeled — the 345 types live only in spine-events.json. /v1/spineevents is mounted and returns count 0. IRI collision: chorus:spine is both this Product and a Domain.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-directing ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-spine-product-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:events ;
    chorus:promise """The Spine is the team's memory of what happened. It is an append-only record of every event the system and the people in it give off — a card pulled, a build finished, a message delivered, a gate refusing an action — together with a catalogue of every kind of event that can happen.
Three promises, in the order they matter. Nothing is lost: the log is append-only and never rotated; disk is cheaper than a forgotten month. Anything recorded is reachable: a question about last Tuesday should be answerable at the speed of a query. What can happen is enumerated: the kinds of event are a curated vocabulary, not whatever strings writers happen to emit.
Everyone reads it: the owner, the roles, and every other product — the pipeline, the observation layer, the vital signs, the operating model and the model itself all read the Spine.""" ;
    chorus:structure """The Spine is a product at the Directing step, in the operating state, grown out of the Clearing into a product of its own. It carries one domain: events, the grammar of what counts as a valid event.
The owner's framing: think of it as an event-driven architecture — there are types of events, and there are many instances of each. Those are two layers. The type layer is a curated vocabulary of several hundred event kinds, each with a category, a value-stream step, and the fields it carries. The instance layer is the stream itself, millions of lines, one per occurrence, each naming its type.
The structural rule that makes it one product: an occurrence is of exactly one type, and that link resolves in both directions — from a line in the log to its definition, and from a definition to everything that ever happened under it.""" ;
    chorus:model """Two classes and one join. An EventType is the vocabulary entry: its dotted name, its category, the step it belongs to, the fields it carries, and which writer declares it. A SpineEvent is one occurrence: when, by whom, under which trace and work item, with its payload — projected on read from the log, not materialized as stored entries.
Why not store every occurrence in the graph: a triple store is the wrong home for a write-once time stream, and millions of subjects would put the entire model inside the blast radius of any accidental wipe. The log stays the log; the model describes it and joins to it.
Alongside: the writer's contract (what a component declares it will emit, which the observation layer reads to build monitors), and the categories that run parallel to the value stream — lifecycle, coordination, security, telemetry. An older, deprecated step vocabulary is being retired.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """The rule is the same as every generated page: render the class's API, link along real relationships, start from the value stream.
Today: the product page you are reading. Planned: an event-types page — the catalogue, browsable by category and step, each type showing its fields and its recent occurrences; an occurrences view that answers a time-window question rather than a byte-count one; and the class atlas already shows the two-property occurrence shape honestly.
The flow of an event: a component emits one line through the shared emit command → the line lands in the log → readers (the vital signs, the observation layer, the pipeline, the board, the agent envelope) tail it → a query or a page joins the line to its type.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Today: one emit command that appends an event; query tools that read the log by work item, by trace, or for recent errors; the recent tail at /api/chorus/context/spine; and a generated read for occurrences that currently serves only the two-property shape.
Planned: /eventtypes — the catalogue, served from the model, loaded from the registry file that holds it today; /spineevents as a real projection over the log with the type join in both directions; a windowed read (a day, an hour) rather than a fixed tail; and a checkable correspondence between the agent tools and the event types, so "every tool leaves an event" is a claim the system can verify in both directions.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Nothing is lost: the log has been append-only and unrotated since mid-2026, and holds millions of lines across more than a month.
Reach is broken. Readers take a fixed tail — a few megabytes, a few hours — of a multi-gigabyte log, so a question about anything older than this morning cannot be answered by the running surfaces. The metrics route over the tail reports zero movement whenever nothing happened in the last hour or two.
The type layer is not yet modeled: the several hundred event kinds live only in a registry file, and the generated occurrence read serves a two-property stub. The deprecated step vocabulary still has a few rows waiting to go.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Make the product real: the product entry, its domain link, and this design served from the model — done.
Split the layers: model the event type, load the vocabulary from the registry into the model, serve the catalogue, and turn the occurrence read into a real projection with the type join both ways.
Restore reach: an index from day to offset in the log, so a reader asks for a window instead of a byte count — which is also what lets the occurrence API answer anything older than this morning.
Close the tool-to-event correspondence: with types modeled, check every agent tool against every event type and report holes in both directions.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Log aggregation, shipping and dashboards — the logs domain of the observation layer.
The writer's contract in detail (adapters, envelope, trace lifecycle) — the logs service design.
The other meaning of "spine" — the value-stream architecture diagram that uses the word as a metaphor.
Ingesting every occurrence into the graph — rejected by design.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/spine-product-design.html (identity, promises, the two layers, composition, the model, pages, API, measured state — 2026-09-01).
Internal notes for the team: the Spine service design (contracts, readers, retention, the measured reach gap); the logs service design (the writer's contract); chorus-spine.html (the value-stream sense of the word); designing/schemas/spine-events.json (the registry that holds the types today). Cards: #4045 (the product, and design sections as attributes), #4046 (types and instances), #4044 (service design), #3697 (step vocabulary deprecated).
Chorus · Spine · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" .

chorus:werk a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Werk""" ;
    rdfs:label """Werk""" ;
    chorus:comment """The Chorus execution protocol that carries a unit of work from pulled to proven-and-accepted, across the Building and Proving steps.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """The Chorus execution protocol that carries a unit of work from pulled to proven-and-accepted, across the Building and Proving steps.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-kade ;
    chorus:vision """Generated domain APIs over the graph end the reconcile treadmill structurally — typed contracts at every boundary, every card a coherent SKU.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Every accepted card leaves as merged source on origin/main with a coherent receipt — one trace from pull to deployed cdhash — so a green result can't lie and work arrives proven rather than taken on faith.""" ;
    chorus:audience """The three roles building Chorus and Gathering plus Jeff accepting the work; the external customer never touches Werk.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """werk-test is held behind the generated tests-domain API; the tests domain lacks per-test identity and result ingestion; card-acceptance does not yet advance artifact status; advisory test and review steps are not yet gates.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-building ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-werk-product-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:tests, chorus:code, chorus:version-control, chorus:cicd, chorus:pipelines ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:promise """Anyone can point an AI coding assistant at a problem and get code that says it is done. What that gives you is a claim. Werk is the part of Chorus that turns the claim into proof: the change is running, it has been shown working, and someone accountable said yes.
Working alone, you are the proof step yourself. The assistant says done; the old binary is still serving; you find out when it breaks in front of someone. Werk closes that gap with one trace from the moment work is picked up to the exact binary that is running, a check at every step, and gates that refuse early — so a green result cannot lie.
The promise to the people who operate it: every piece of work that enters leaves as merged source with a coherent receipt. Werk owns two steps of the value stream — Building, where intent becomes running software, and Proving, where it is shown and accepted. The steps before them decide what to build. And it is versioned: every response from every role opens with the protocol version it is running under, so drift between roles is visible at a glance.""" ;
    chorus:structure """Werk is a top-level product at the Building step, in the operating state. It is made of five domains: pipelines (one or more instances, each owned by the role whose work it carries, all conforming to one set of contracts), version control (the verb binaries, an isolated workspace per piece of work, guards, conflict holds, recovery), code (the quality rules enforced before a commit — lint, complexity, duplication), tests (test-first discipline, hermetic by default, integration explicitly gated), and the build-and-run instance itself. Werk consumes a sibling product for building and deploying artifacts; that hand-off is the boundary of what it owns.
Its interface is a family of twelve verbs, each a small self-contained program: pull, commit, push, build, test, review, deploy, demo, merge, sync, accept, unpull. Each has its own lock, refuses with a typed reason, leaves a witness line and an event, and is reachable as an agent tool and as a status check on the pull request.
Its surfaces: the version stamp at the top of every response, the one pipeline trigger, the five demo gates plus an adversarial review, the pull request with one check per verb, the event log, and the board.""" ;
    chorus:model """The rule for the model is query-backwards: every class and property must map to data that already exists or has a defined path in — witness lines, events, verb runs, gate results, review verdicts, work items. A class that cannot answer a query is decoration and is cut.
Eighteen classes. Shared: a VerbRun (one execution of a verb for a piece of work — the atom of Werk's observability), a TypedRefusal, a Witness stream per verb. Pipeline: a PipelineRun, a GateResult (five kinds; all five required before a demo may be presented), a ReviewVerdict, a DemoVerdict (the human decision; the go is the acceptance), a Deployment carrying the signed identity of the binary so "deployed" provably equals "running". Version control: a WerkSlot (the isolated workspace), a MergeEvent, a ConflictHold, a RecoveryAction. Code: a SourceFile, a CardDiff, a QualityFinding. Tests: a Test with durable identity, a TestSuiteRun, a TestResult.
Eight relationships (a work item's verb runs, gate results, review and demo verdicts; a test's file, what it covers, what scenario it verifies) and six properties. Twenty-two shapes written with the model: every class requires at least its identity, and every link is checked at write time, because the query language fails silently on broken chains.
The tests domain is live and consumed: thousands of test entries with coverage links; the test verb derives its run plan from them and writes each run back.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """One workflow file, run as one job, with a stop in the middle. Commit → push → build → test (blocking) → review → deploy to an isolated environment → bring that environment up (its own API, its own store, its own Clearing, on its own ports) → demo: present and stop. Then, on the owner's go: merge → sync → deploy to production → accept. An interrupted run resumes; steps already done report themselves idempotent rather than failing.
The operator's loop: pull (the work item moves to in-progress and gets a fresh workspace from the main branch) → build in the workspace → run the pipeline to the demo stop → the owner looks at the running environment — a demo is try-before-you-buy; no go without seeing the change working → go, no, or more → the go is the acceptance, and the workspace is torn down. Two touches per work item is the target.
Pages: the cockpit at the go decision (verbs, gates, tests, review, environment), the work-state dashboard, a where-is-each-pipeline view, the pull request with one status per verb, and the event log by work item or trace.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """For agents: one typed tool per verb — pull, commit, push, build, deploy, review, merge, accept, unpull — and one trigger that runs the pipeline to the demo stop, or, with an explicit go, past it to acceptance. The tool surface is pinned by a contract test; adding a verb is a deliberate act.
The verb programs themselves are installed to one location after being built, signed and verified, so the same source always yields the same installed identity and system permissions survive rebuilds. Each takes the work item and the role, refuses with a typed reason (no workspace, wrong status, no open pull request, gates missing, no acceptance criteria, rejected ceremony), and writes a witness line.
Reads: the generated tests domain (what the test verb consumes), the pipelines and pipeline-steps reads, the events (pulled, demoed, accepted, built, deploy identity matched, test plan degraded), and the pull-request status checks.
Guards: edits outside the work item's workspace are refused; test and build contexts are refused writes to production surfaces; lint and complexity ratchets run before every commit.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Shipped: the full twelve-verb family; one workflow, one run, with the go given at invocation; the go is the acceptance; atomic execution across the family; the test verb blocking and model-driven, with a run plan derived from coverage and every degradation recorded; resume-aware steps; native workspace teardown; a store of detached runs; isolated per-work-item workspaces; conflict hold, continue and abort; merge by content with idempotent re-land; error-tier lint and complexity at pre-commit; a test-value taxonomy applied with teeth; an isolated environment per demo with its own store and Clearing; skipped tests counted as skipped, not failed.
Product gaps closed: try before you buy (every change demos live before the go); receipts reconcile (one trace threads both halves); deployed equals running (proved by binary identity); tests as a real gate.
Open: per-test result ingestion (suite grain only today); a write token for the runner environment; artifact status not advanced on acceptance, so the model drifts behind reality; review advisory by design; rollback across version control, build and deploy structurally hard. And a habit the pipeline exposes rather than causes: presenting a change with "one honest gap" and asking for a go — not demoable means not presented.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Advance artifact status on acceptance: when a change to a service is accepted, write the status transition through the model's API as part of landing, so the model cannot drift behind reality.
Per-test result grain and a write token in the runner environment; then drop the legacy test lanes once a week of model-planned runs proves the coverage-based plan.
Carry the work-item identity across the scheduler boundary, so post-acceptance events stop appearing as system events and can be correlated by work item.
Model Werk's own domains under the shared structural model.
A sibling trigger for model changes sharing the same demo, go and accept tail; workspace teardown that can never remove a presented environment; run numbers per work item; and a test runner shaped by domain (work item → domain → unit, page, integration, security) rather than by tool.
How Werk knows it is working: cycle time, lead time, escaping defects and time to recover all trend the right way, and false-green acceptances trend to zero.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Building and deploying artifacts — a sibling product; Werk consumes it and hands off at the post-acceptance release trigger.
The structural model itself — Werk conforms to it, does not redefine it.
The board and the work-item lifecycle — the Clearing's; Werk consumes the state machine.
The personal application's own continuous integration — lives in its own repository.
Quality of the running system — the observation layer's.
A staging environment — rejected: there is one production, and the isolated per-work-item environment is the demo.""" ;
    chorus:references """Public design: designing/docs/werk-product-design.html (why pay rather than just prompt; the product; surfaces; the five domains; work-item and artifact coherence; the query-backwards model; path to close — reconciled 2026-07-12); werk-value-stream-design.html (the twelve-verb cycle and its engine).
Internal notes for the team: werk-review-service-design.html; tests-service-design.html; version-control-service-design.html; ci-pipeline-service-design.html; quality-service-design.html; build-and-deploy-service-design.html (the sibling); werk-pipeline-model.html, werk-v2-tobe.html, werk-process.html. Decisions: ADR-037 (atomic verbs; one environment; the pull request as process holder), ADR-032 (verb contract), ADR-031, ADR-036, ADR-026, ADR-053 (the merge gate), DEC-048 (the go is the land authority), DEC-1674 (test first), DEC-2525. Cards: #3499 (one run), #3311 (go is accept), #3190 / #3634 (test verb blocking, model-driven), #3632 (resume), #3431 (teardown), #2913 (isolated workspaces), #3222 / #3232 (binary identity), #4075 (environment per demo), #4078 (skips counted), #4062 (model trigger), #2734 (one install location).
Chorus · Werk · rewritten for readers outside the team 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .
